3. Methodology

The research follows a mixed‑methods approach combining simulation‑in‑the‑loop experiments and physical validation, structured into five work packages (WPs):

WP Research Question Method Independent Variables Dependent Variables Controls
WP1 RQ1 DTiL simulation (ROS2‑Gazebo + AirSim) + 1:20 scale physical mock‑up Drone count (1→5), comms bandwidth, GPS‑denied vs RTK Point‑cloud registration RMSE (mm), mesh completeness (%) Baseline: Leica RTC360 terrestrial laser scanner
WP2 RQ2 PD‑VNN vs. DBN vs. FEM on multi‑flight‑cycle dataset Model architecture, training dataset size, damage mode Crack‑length prediction RMSE (mm), remaining useful life (cycles) Ground truth: X‑ray CT of test panels
WP3 RQ3 LLM + ASKG + deterministic verifier vs. threshold‑based logic LLM architecture, knowledge‑graph completeness, verifier strictness False‑positive rate (%), recall (%), engineer‑time savings (min) Ground truth: labelled defect catalogue
WP4 RQ4 Graceful‑degradation stress tests Comms protocol (6G → 5G → 4G), packet‑loss rate Registration drift (mm), re‑scan trigger rate Identical test scenario across all protocols
WP5 Integration Full‑scale virtual inspection campaign End‑to‑end pipeline with all five drones Total inspection time, launch‑readiness score accuracy Current manual inspection baseline (from published timelines)

Each work package contains explicitly stated hypotheses. For example, H1: A heterogeneous five‑drone swarm operating under RTK‑GPS will achieve point‑cloud registration accuracy of ≤ 2 mm RMSE on a stainless‑steel cylinder at 5–8 m standoff, statistically indistinguishable from a terrestrial laser scanner baseline (α = 0.05). A null result (RMSE > 5 mm in the GPS‑denied metallic‑interference condition) would constitute a failure of the hypothesis and would force a fundamental re‑design of the SMD SLAM pipeline.

3.1 Simulation Environment

Validation will be conducted in a Digital Twin‑in‑the‑Loop (DTiL) environment using an open‑source ROS2‑Gazebo‑ArduPilot simulation stack. A 1:20 scale Starship mock‑up (6 m tall, 0.45 m diameter, SS304) will be constructed for physical validation, instrumented with known artificial defects (EDM‑notched cracks of 0.5–5 mm depth, simulated delaminations, and thermal voids). Simulation will handle the full 120 m scale. A published ROS‑Gazebo‑ArduPilot framework has demonstrated realistic environment reconstruction and multi‑UAV trajectory tracking.

3.2 Statistical Framework

All experiments will use a minimum of 30 independent trials per condition to achieve statistical power. Results will be reported with 95 % confidence intervals. Comparative analyses (SMD vs. terrestrial scanner, PD‑VNN vs. DBN) will use two‑sided paired t‑tests or Wilcoxon signed‑rank tests with Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons.

