5. System Coordination Architecture

5.1 Swarm Control with LiDAR‑Based Monitor Drone

Collision avoidance in the GPS‑denied, metallic launch‑pad environment is handled by a LiDAR‑based monitor‑drone framework: a dedicated drone (or the SMD, dynamically assigned) uses airborne 3D object detection with sparse convolution to track all swarm members, achieving >80 % recall and >96 % precision. Adaptive re‑scan triggering dispatches higher‑resolution follow‑ups when the anomaly detector returns “uncertain.”

5.2 6G Semantic Communication with Formal Graceful Degradation

The primary communication layer is a 6G‑enabled semantic communication architecture, in which each drone encodes multimodal sensor data into task‑relevant semantic representations before transmission. However, recognising that 6G is not commercially deployed at any launch facility as of 2026, the proposal includes an explicit graceful‑degradation path:

Tier Protocol Throughput Operational Consequence
Tier 1 6G URLLC + semantic coding ≥1 Gbps Full operation: all modalities streamed, real‑time digital twin
Tier 2 5G sub‑6 GHz + semantic coding ~150 Mbps Reduced operation: thermal + visual modalities; LiDAR down‑sampled to 25 %
Tier 3 4G LTE ~50 Mbps Degraded operation: semantic defect vectors only; raw data buffered on‑drone; digital twin reconstructed post‑mission
Tier 4 UWB mesh only ~6.8 Mbps Safety mode: telemetry + critical anomaly flags only; all drones return to base

The specific throughput threshold at which registration drift exceeds 5 mm will be empirically determined in WP4.

5.3 Edge‑Fog‑Cloud Data Pipeline

· Edge (on‑drone): Image preprocessing, feature extraction, local odometry.
· Fog (launch‑pad server): Point‑cloud registration, mesh generation, CAD comparison, PD‑VNN inference.
· Cloud (post‑inspection): Long‑term storage, federated weight aggregation, fleet‑wide trend analysis.

